== 1.8 Chapter 1 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [Literature is best defined as language organised so that],
  [the way it is said carries meaning],
  [its subject matter is important],
  [it records true events],
  [it can be read aloud]
)

#mcq(2, [Which of these is NOT a reason for calling a text literature?],
  [it asks the reader to interpret],
  [it is printed in a book],
  [its language is chosen rather than merely used],
  [its form carries meaning]
)

#mcq(3, [A memoir can be literature while a fabricated news story is not, because literature is not defined by],
  [truth of content],
  [length],
  [the reputation of the writer],
  [the language used]
)

#mcq(4, [The three genres of literature are],
  [fiction, non-fiction and drama],
  [drama, prose and poetry],
  [tragedy, comedy and farce],
  [novel, novella and short story]
)

#mcq(5, [A genre is best described as],
  [a theme common to many works],
  [a writer's personal style],
  [a family of texts sharing a way of reaching the reader],
  [a period in literary history]
)

#mcq(6, [The evidence a candidate may quote from a play includes],
  [stage directions, dialogue and entrances],
  [the narrator's comments],
  [the poet's biography],
  [the chapter headings]
)

#mcq(7, [The question drama principally sets is],
  [who wants what, and who stops them?],
  [what is the poet feeling?],
  [what happened first?],
  [how does the narrator know?]
)

#mcq(8, [The four levels of reading, in order, are],
  [structural, literal, figurative, evaluative],
  [literal, figurative, structural, evaluative],
  [figurative, literal, evaluative, structural],
  [literal, structural, figurative, evaluative]
)

#mcq(9, [“The compound is described as a garment, something worn and then put off.” This observation belongs to],
  [the structural level],
  [the literal level],
  [the evaluative level],
  [the figurative level]
)

#mcq(10, [“The extract withholds the elders' demand until after her refusal.” This observation belongs to],
  [the literal level],
  [the figurative level],
  [the structural level],
  [the evaluative level]
)

#mcq(11, [An evaluative reading of a text asks],
  [how the text is arranged],
  [what happens],
  [how well it works, and at what cost],
  [what the images suggest]
)

#mcq(12, [A full literary answer contains],
  [the device and its definition],
  [the effect alone],
  [the device, the evidence and the effect],
  [the evidence alone]
)

#mcq(13, [“The poet uses a metaphor” earns little credit because],
  [it is written in the wrong tense],
  [it could describe almost any poem],
  [it misnames the device],
  [it quotes inaccurately]
)

#mcq(14, [The part of a literary answer that a mark scheme chiefly rewards is],
  [the length of the response],
  [the quotation],
  [the name of the device],
  [the effect and its consequence]
)

#mcq(15, [The theme of a work differs from its subject in that the theme],
  [is shorter to state],
  [is always stated by the author],
  [concerns only the setting],
  [makes a statement about the subject]
)

#mcq(16, [Tone is to mood as],
  [the writer's attitude is to the reader's feeling],
  [the setting is to the plot],
  [sound is to sense],
  [the speaker is to the poet]
)

#mcq(17, [Diction refers to],
  [the overall manner of a writer],
  [the choice of words],
  [the arrangement of chapters],
  [the rhythm of a sentence]
)

#mcq(18, [The speaker of a poem is],
  [the poem's dedicatee],
  [always the poet],
  [the reader],
  [the voice constructed within the text]
)

#mcq(19, [The difference between story and plot is that plot],
  [contains no dialogue],
  [is shorter],
  [belongs only to drama],
  [is the order in which the text presents events]
)

#mcq(20, [A question beginning “The device used in line 4 is …” is testing],
  [definition],
  [identification],
  [context],
  [evaluation]
)

#mcq(21, [A question beginning “The effect of the repetition is to …” sends you to],
  [the poet's biography],
  [the dictionary],
  [the change between the first and last repetition],
  [the title only]
)

#mcq(22, [A question asking what a passage is “chiefly concerned with” is best answered from],
  [the punctuation],
  [the longest sentence],
  [the first line],
  [the last line]
)

#mcq(23, [In the sentence “He was a lion in the fight, but a lamb at home”, the turn marked by *but* signals],
  [a new character],
  [a shift in tone or evaluation],
  [a change of subject],
  [a stage direction]
)

#mcq(24, [A play opens with *A room. A chair. Rain at the window.* The function of this opening is mainly],
  [to provide comic relief],
  [to establish mood and expectation before any word is spoken],
  [to identify the characters],
  [to state the theme]
)

#mcq(25, [Which observation is at the literal level?],
  [The widow refuses to leave the compound],
  [The compound stands for the marriage],
  [The image makes grief a matter of clothing],
  [The scene delays the elders' arrival]
)

#mcq(26, [The best evidence that a text is poetry rather than prose is],
  [it has a title],
  [its lines are units of composition, not accidents of the margin],
  [it rhymes],
  [it uses imagery]
)

#mcq(27, [Which statement about a prose text would a mark scheme treat as structural rather than literal?],
  [The market is crowded],
  [The narrator dislikes markets],
  [The narrator describes a market],
  [The market description precedes the private conversation and the unattributed closing line]
)

#mcq(28, [An answer that names a device and quotes it but stops there has supplied],
  [name and evidence],
  [name and effect],
  [effect only],
  [evidence and effect]
)

#mcq(29, [“The poem's tone is bitter; its mood is one of defeat.” This statement is],
  [correct, because tone names the writer's attitude and mood the reader's feeling],
  [wrong, because mood belongs only to drama],
  [correct, because tone and mood are synonyms],
  [wrong, because a poem cannot have a mood]
)

#mcq(30, [“The diction of the passage is simple” is a claim about],
  [the length of the passage],
  [the narrator's reliability],
  [the arrangement of sentences],
  [the selection of words]
)

#mcq(31, [Which of these is a statement of theme rather than of subject?],
  [A community punishes the woman it claims to protect],
  [The death of a husband],
  [A compound in a village],
  [Widowhood]
)

#mcq(32, [The six question shapes in the paper are definition, identification, interpretation, effect, context and],
  [summary],
  [translation],
  [evaluation],
  [spelling]
)

#mcq(33, [A student writes: “The writer uses repetition to emphasise.” The weakness of this answer is that it],
  [misidentifies the device],
  [names an intention instead of showing an effect],
  [uses too many words],
  [quotes the wrong line]
)

#mcq(34, [Which question could NOT be answered from a single extract without knowing the whole work?],
  [Where in the novel does this chapter fall?],
  [What is the tone of the closing sentence?],
  [What does the image of the garment suggest?],
  [What does the speaker's refusal reveal?]
)

#mcq(35, [Literature is often called an art of *selection* because],
  [it chooses famous subjects],
  [it is edited before publication],
  [it selects its readers],
  [it uses only some of the available words and details to produce its effect]
)

#mcq(36, [In marking, a candidate who produces a brilliant point about the wrong line is usually awarded],
  [marks for the quality of the handwriting],
  [little or nothing, because the evidence does not support the point],
  [full marks],
  [half marks for effort]
)

#mcq(37, [The most economical way to raise a literal answer to a structural one is to ask],
  [how long is this?],
  [what does this mean?],
  [who wrote this?],
  [why is this placed here rather than elsewhere?]
)

#mcq(38, [A candidate who answers “the mood is gloomy” when the stem asks for the *tone* has most likely],
  [confused diction with style],
  [confused story with plot],
  [named the audience's feeling rather than the writer's attitude],
  [mistaken the speaker for the poet]
)

#mcq(39, [Which pair is correctly distinguished?],
  [Subject: what it is about; theme: what it says about that],
  [Subject: the narrator; theme: the author],
  [Subject: the moral; theme: the topic],
  [Subject: the setting; theme: the plot]
)

#mcq(40, [The single most reliable habit for an unseen extract is to],
  [look for the longest word],
  [read it twice and underline the verb in the stem],
  [count the lines],
  [memorise the author's dates]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

